  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 137 PM CDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nPrecipitation/Storm Trends...A few showers and perhaps a storm\n(only ~500 J/kg of CAPE to work with) will continue across\nnorthern WI this afternoon as a 850mb warm front/low-level WAA \ngradient gradually lift northeast across the region. Can't rule \nout some isolated activity along/near the warm front further \nsouth, but the capping inversion will likely prohibit much\nactivity from occurring. The area of showers/storms over \nnortheast Iowa associated with a shortwave and LLJ could also clip\nthe far southeast later this afternoon. For tonight, the majority\nof the shower/storm activity looks to focus just north of the \narea, mainly associated with the nose of the LLJ and MUCAPE \ngradient as it shifts northward. That area could clip far northern\nWI overnight. Locally heavy downpours (impressive PWATs near 2\") \nand small hail would be the main concern of any elevated storm.\n\nOn Monday, there will be a conditional threat for strong to severe\nstorms as plenty of instability/shear (MUCAPE 2000-3000 J/kg and \ndeep-layer shear of 30-40 kts) in place, a weak frontal boundary \nand surface low sagging across the area, and a weak shortwave/jet \noverhead. However, the capping inversion looks to still be in \nplace for much of the day. Question will be whether it will break \nduring peak heating to allow for the storms to form. CAMs not much\nhelp due to the cap, with models flip-flopping around each run. \nHRRR Neural Network showing some probabilities near 10% now across\ncentral and east central WI, signaling the scenario if the cap \ncan break. Other ML/AI guidance paints a small threat for severe \nstorms across central/eastern WI. SPC has expanded the Marginal \nRisk into much of the area due to this threat, which is \nreasonable. Main threats would be damaging winds and hail, along \nwith torrential downpours. Some elevated convection could linger \ninto Monday night, but coverage looks limited. Tuesday should \nstart off dry, but chances for additional showers and storms \nreturns later in the day into Tuesday night as the boundary will \nbe hovering to our south. A couple shortwaves then look to move \nover the region through the remainder of the work week, bringing \nintermittent chances for showers and storms at times.\n\nTemperatures/Humidity...After a warm and somewhat humid day today,\ntemps will be even warmer on Monday and Tuesday with highs in the\nmiddle 80s to lower 90s. The persistent southerly flow will\ncontinue to usher higher dewpoints as well, increasing into the \nupper 60s and low 70s. Heat indices in the 90s are expected on \nMonday, with a few of the hotter spots getting close to 100, if\ntemps or dewpoints slightly over perform.",
